FactionBackstory (
    faction_id: "starlight",
    canonical_name: "The Starlight Concordant",
    motto: "We chart the unknown.",
    summary: "An interstellar exploration consortium chartered to map the outer system, pioneers of the Mimas and Europa biosphere surveys. Deep navy vacuum suits with starlight-yellow trim; constellation patches sewn on shoulders; stay alive long enough to bring the data home.",
    history: "Starlight was chartered in 2118 by the Lagrange Concordat as an independent exploration body, with the explicit task of mapping the outer Sol system before the Frontier or the Collegium got there first. The first Starlight expedition, the Voyager-Charter, mapped the inner Belt and lost two of seven ships. The second mapped Phobos and Deimos and lost three of six. The fourth, in 2126, made the first Concordat-recognized contact with the Crystalfold and lost nobody, which is a result Starlight has not duplicated.\n\nFor a generation Starlight was a small body of pilots, navigators, and survey specialists, funded irregularly by Concordat subsidy and Collegium grants. The Coalition tolerated them. The Frontier robbed them. The Collegium hired them. By 2160, Starlight had completed the first full biosphere survey of Mimas and the first cavern survey of Europa, and had established a reputation for going further, returning later, and bringing back more data per surviving crewman than any other body in the system. Starlight ships are unarmed by charter; in practice, they carry the kind of long-range defensive armament that makes Coalition cruisers nervous.\n\nThe Mimas survey of 2168 produced the first contact with a methane-breather settlement; the Concordant declared the methane-breathers a sentient species in 2171, over a Coalition objection that took six years to resolve. Starlight has, since that point, been the standing diplomatic body for first contact across the outer system. Crews are trained to avoid combat at all costs, then to escalate to overwhelming force without warning if avoidance fails. The doctrine has saved more lives than it has cost; more importantly, it has produced data that has saved more lives than it has saved.\n\nThe modern Concordant numbers approximately four thousand active personnel across one hundred and twelve hulls. Starlight ships are recognizable on radar by their abnormally large auxiliary power plants, abnormally heavy comm rigs, and abnormally long-range relay antennas. Starlight crews are recognizable in person by their constellation patches, their tendency to speak in three languages at once, and their absolute, unshakable belief that the next thing they will find will be the one that matters.",
    philosophy: "We chart the unknown. The Concordant believes that information is the only weapon that compounds, and that the universe contains more information than has been recorded, and that the gap is the work. Crews are trained to document everything, defend nothing, and withdraw to safe extraction the moment the data is secured. Starlight officers do not seek glory; Starlight officers seek the next survey, which is the only thing the Concordant considers worth seeking.",
    relationships: [
        FactionRelation (other: "coalition", stance: "operational_alliance", notes: "Starlight ships fly under Coalition flag for legal cover; the Coalition uses Starlight data to plan responses to anomalies before the Frontier or the Collegium gets there. Polite, mutual, durable. Both sides describe the arrangement as 'overdue for renegotiation' and neither side has scheduled the meeting."),
        FactionRelation (other: "frontier", stance: "covert_rivalry", notes: "Starlight maps anomalies; the Frontier follows the maps. Starlight does not press the issue, because the Frontier does the work Starlight charters explicitly forbid. Starlight survey logs occasionally include red-pencil notes that the Frontier finds extremely useful and Starlight pretends it did not leave."),
        FactionRelation (other: "ronin", stance: "courtesy_alliance", notes: "Each Starlight survey ship carries one Ronin master by ancestral arrangement with House Yamamoto. The master rides as a passenger and rarely intervenes. When they do intervene, the survey is complete, the ship is moving, and the report contains a line item nobody will ever ask about."),
        FactionRelation (other: "synth", stance: "operational_alliance", notes: "Starlight survey data is the primary input to the Synth anomaly model. The Synth Collective subsidizes Starlight expeditions through a Coalition-laundered contract. Neither side discusses this; both sides have priced it in."),
        FactionRelation (other: "collective", stance: "operational_alliance", notes: "Starlight maps Crystalfold settlements as protected, delivers crystalline seeds to new Sol-zone habitats, and refuses to renegotiate the arrangement under any circumstance. The Crystalfold call Starlight ships 'the carriers of the new'. Starlight crews call the Crystalfold settlements 'home, for tonight'."),
        FactionRelation (other: "husks", stance: "avoidance_pact", notes: "Starlight survey routes do not enter Husk territory. The Husks do not interfere with Starlight ships in transit. The arrangement is not written down. It has held for thirty-one years and is, by Starlight standards, the most successful diplomatic agreement in the Concordant's history."),
        FactionRelation (other: "collegium", stance: "joint_charter", notes: "Starlight survey ships are co-chartered with the Collegium on long expeditions; the Collegium provides instruments and Starlight provides the ship. Findings are divided by treaty. Both sides claim the better share; the arrangement holds because neither side has the leverage to renegotiate without losing the relationship entirely."),
    ],
    territory: "No fixed territory. Starlight maintains landing-pad and refueling rights at one hundred and twelve sites across the Sol system, including every Coalition outpost, every Crystalfold settlement, and three Belt asteroids whose flag the Concordant refuses to specify. The Concordant's nominal headquarters is on Lagrange-7 station and is, in practice, used for nothing but the storage of survey logs and the preparation of the annual budget.",
    aesthetic: "Deep navy and starlight-yellow vacuum suits, with constellation patches sewn on the shoulders — a personal patch for every world the wearer has surveyed, accumulated over a career. Helmets are streamlined, with broad faceplates and a yellow-tinted visor that reduces solar glare. Survey rigs are reinforced at the spine and the back-of-thigh, where the auxiliary battery pack rides. Officers carry a heavy survey staff that doubles as a stadia rod and, in extremis, as a rifle.",
    voice_characteristics: "Calm. Conversational. Slightly accented by long isolation; Starlight crews develop fleet dialects that shift over the course of a long expedition and never quite reset. NPCs speak in measured, deliberate sentences, use technical vocabulary by reflex, and frequently break off mid-sentence to record an observation. Officers are quiet. Pilots are talkative. Survey specialists are, by reputation, both at once. The Concordant's diplomats are the only people in the Sol system who can have a polite conversation with a Husk; the Concordant has not asked them how."
)
